menu "ESP32 Device SDK"

    config GADGET_PRODUCT_NAME
        string "Human-visible product name for log messages"
        default "Muse Gadget"
        help
            The product name shown in boot banners, log messages, and
            diagnostic text. Does not affect protocol identifiers or
            backend strings. Internal builds override this to match
            the shipping product name.

    config GADGET_SDK_TOKEN
        string "Muse Gadgets SDK token"
        default ""
        help
            Your mgst_ SDK token from gadgets.muse.ai (Account > SDK tokens).
            Every gadget needs one to pair. The device hands it to the Muse
            app inside the encrypted pairing session and includes it when it
            refreshes its device token.

    config GADGET_SDK_TOKEN_WARN
        bool "Warn on every build when no SDK token is set"
        default y
        help
            Print a prominent warning during each build while
            CONFIG_GADGET_SDK_TOKEN is empty. The build still succeeds.
            Internal builds that intentionally ship without an SDK token
            turn this off.

    config HOMEHUB_WIFI_SSID
        string "WiFi SSID override (dev iteration)"
        default ""
        help
            If non-empty, the firmware connects to this SSID at boot
            without waiting for a BLE provisioning command. Useful for
            iterating on tunnel throughput without re-pairing each flash.
            BLE still follows setup state: it only advertises when setup is
            incomplete. If no device token pair is in NVS, the tunnel stays
            down and the device waits for BLE provisioning.

    config HOMEHUB_WIFI_PASSWORD
        string "WiFi password override"
        default ""
        help
            Password for CONFIG_HOMEHUB_WIFI_SSID. Ignored when the SSID
            override is empty.

    config HOMEHUB_TOKEN_REFRESH_INTERVAL_DAYS
        int "Proactive device token refresh interval (days, 0 = disabled)"
        range 0 365
        default 30
        help
            Safety net against the access token's real lifetime being shorter
            than the 365 days the API service advertises. Normal operation refreshes
            reactively, only when a token is refused; this adds a slow periodic
            refresh on top.

            Measured against UPTIME, not the calendar — the device has no clock
            and NVS here stores strings only. A device that reboots more often
            than this interval never triggers it, which is fine: every boot
            re-validates the token via fetch_vms and a 401 there drives the
            reactive path. This exists for devices that stay up for months.

            Each refresh is one call to the endpoint that can mint unusable
            tokens after an idle period, so keep this long. Set 0 to disable.

    config HOMEHUB_OTA_ENABLED
        bool "Enable OTA firmware updates"
        default n
        help
            Allow firmware updates over authenticated control and BLE setup.
            When disabled, OTA commands are not advertised and update requests
            are skipped without downloading, even when force is requested.
            Optional OTA parameters in Wi-Fi setup are ignored so provisioning
            can finish normally. Internal builds enable this explicitly.

    config HOMEHUB_SUPPORT_BUG_REPORT
        bool "Enable private support bug reporting"
        default n
        help
            Accept the private bug.report command over authenticated remote
            control. Do not advertise it in generic node.register commands.
            The report can contain restricted support PII from the bounded
            current-boot application log, but never credentials,
            keys, signed URL queries, or arbitrary response bodies.
            The dedicated support path forwards the opaque JSON through the
            configured report endpoint as a private attachment
            and suppresses the payload from user-visible chat. Supported
            profiles enable this explicitly; the Kconfig fallback remains off
            for custom builds that bypass the supported sdkconfig defaults.

    config HOMEHUB_SHOW_AGENT_NAME
        bool "Show the agent's name on the display"
        default y if HOMEHUB_DISPLAY
        help
            After the device registers, fetch GET /identity over the Noise
            session and show the agent's name on the status display. Only
            display backends can show it, so it is off for LED-only builds.

    config HOMEHUB_TUNNEL
        bool "Enable the home-network tunnel and network discovery"
        default y
        help
            Open the /link-tunnel data-plane stream on the Noise session, bring
            up the NAPT tunnel netif, and accept device.discover. Together they
            need roughly 60 KB of internal RAM beyond the control session, plus
            up to 256 KB for discovery results, so boards without PSRAM (e.g.
            the classic ESP32) should disable this. The control API to the VM
            keeps working; the status turns green once the control session is up.

    config HOMEHUB_BLE_PERSISTENT
        bool "Keep BLE running next to Wi-Fi and the Noise session"
        depends on BT_NIMBLE_ENABLED && !MUSE_ENABLED
        default n
        help
            By default the NimBLE stack is shut down and its memory released
            once setup completes, so BLE never shares the radio or internal RAM
            with Wi-Fi and the VM session. With this on, the stack stays up
            after setup: setup advertising stops, and a basic GATT service
            (main/ble_basic.c) is advertised slowly so a phone or hub can
            still find and connect to the gadget. Re-pairing still needs the
            long-press reset. Costs the NimBLE controller and host heap
            (roughly 40 KB of internal RAM with the default buffers) for as
            long as the device runs.

    config HOMEHUB_BLE_PERSISTENT_ADV_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Advertising interval after setup (ms)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_BLE_PERSISTENT
        range 100 10240
        default 1000
        help
            Interval for the slow, connectable advertising that keeps the
            device visible after setup. Setup advertising uses NimBLE's fast
            30-60 ms default; every advertising event takes the shared radio
            from Wi-Fi, so a slow interval keeps BLE's share of airtime small.

    config HOMEHUB_BLE_THROUGHPUT_TEST
        bool "BLE throughput test characteristic"
        depends on HOMEHUB_BLE_PERSISTENT
        default n
        help
            Add a characteristic to the basic service for measuring BLE
            throughput beside Wi-Fi: writes without response are counted
            and dropped, and writing a 4-byte little-endian count to it makes
            the device notify that many bytes back. Bench builds only.

    config HOMEHUB_FAST_GCM
        bool "Faster AES-GCM GHASH (chips without a GCM engine)"
        depends on MBEDTLS_HARDWARE_AES && !MBEDTLS_HARDWARE_GCM
        default y if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5 && SPIRAM
        default n
        help
            Measured on the C5 DevKitC-1: AES-256-GCM seal of an 8 KB tunnel
            frame in PSRAM 3.5 ms -> 2.0 ms, and tunnel TX through TLS +30 to
            +66% (tools/coexist/README.md). Validated against IDF's GCM by the
            bench's gcm_verify and tests/test_ghash32.py.
            Wrap ESP-IDF's one-shot AES-GCM (used by TLS records and Noise
            transport messages through PSA) with main/fast_gcm.c: CTR on the
            AES DMA engine as before, GHASH with 32-bit tables cached per key
            instead of IDF's 64-bit 4-bit tables rebuilt on every call.

    config HOMEHUB_FAST_GCM_DEFAULT_MODE
        int "Fast GCM mode at boot (0 off, 1 4-bit tables, 2 8-bit tables)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_FAST_GCM
        range 0 2
        default 2
        help
            8-bit tables take 4 KB of internal RAM per cached key (up to four
            keys, allocated on first use) and halve GHASH's work again.

    config HOMEHUB_FAST_GCM_G8_TABLES
        int "8-bit GHASH tables to keep (4 KB internal RAM each)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_FAST_GCM
        range 0 4
        default 4 if SPIRAM
        default 0
        help
            One per AES key in use: the VM session's TLS link and its Noise
            transport use two each. Keys beyond this use 4-bit tables.

    config HOMEHUB_FAST_GCM_BOUNCE_BYTES
        int "Internal staging buffer for PSRAM data (bytes)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_FAST_GCM
        range 256 16384
        default 2048
        help
            Static internal RAM that PSRAM plaintext and ciphertext pass
            through on their way to the AES engine. Multiple of 16.

    config HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        bool "Data-path and BLE coexistence bench (replaces the app)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_BLE_PERSISTENT
        default n
        help
            Boot into main/pipeline_bench.cpp instead of the gadget: memory
            and AES-GCM micro-benchmarks, then (with HOMEHUB_WIFI_SSID set)
            TCP, TLS and tunnel-pipeline throughput to a host found by UDP
            beacon, and BLE scan/connect/peripheral tests beside Wi-Fi.
            Results print as "BENCH ..." lines. Never ship this.

    config HOMEHUB_BENCH_SECONDS
        int "Seconds per bench phase"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        default 8

    config HOMEHUB_BENCH_QUICK
        bool "Short bench (before/after pipelines only)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        default n

    config HOMEHUB_BENCH_SOAK_MINUTES
        int "Soak for this many minutes instead of the one-shot bench (0 off)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        range 0 1440
        default 0

    config HOMEHUB_BENCH_RX_DELAY_MS
        int "Emulated extra round-trip time on the bench's Wi-Fi link (ms)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        range 0 500
        default 0
        help
            Delays every inbound packet on the station interface, so a LAN
            bench sees a WAN-like round trip (the VM is ~50 ms away). TCP
            window sizes only matter once the round trip is long.

    config HOMEHUB_BENCH_BAND
        int "Wi-Fi band for the bench (0 auto, 1 2.4 GHz, 2 5 GHz)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        range 0 2
        default 0

    config HOMEHUB_BENCH_BEACON_PORT
        int "UDP port the bench host announces itself on"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        default 5200

    config HOMEHUB_BENCH_BLE_PEER
        string "BLE peripheral name the central test connects to"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PIPELINE_BENCH
        default "MuseGadget-A7F5B4"

    config HOMEHUB_DEV_BUILD
        bool "Dev build (dev signing key + dev-only affordances)"
        default y
        help
            Marks this as a development build. Default y, so a plain
            `idf.py build` is a dev build signed with the committed
            dev_signing_key.pem. Internal release builds set this to n and
            point signing at the secret release key.
            Firmware can branch on it with `#if CONFIG_HOMEHUB_DEV_BUILD`.
            Dev builds may log sensitive support details such as network
            identifiers and VM metadata. They must never log credentials,
            authorization headers, signed URL query parameters, private keys,
            or arbitrary response bodies.

    config HOMEHUB_BLE_NAME_PREFIX
        string "Visible BLE name prefix"
        default "MuseGadget"
        help
            The device advertises as "<prefix><suffix>-XXXXXX", where XXXXXX
            is the last three Wi-Fi STA MAC octets. Keep prefix and suffix
            together at most 22 characters so the name fits the BLE scan
            response. The backend node_id ("homelink-XXXXXX") does not change.

    config HOMEHUB_BLE_NAME_SUFFIX
        string "Visible BLE name suffix"
        default ""
        help
            Appended to HOMEHUB_BLE_NAME_PREFIX. Bench boards set one, such as
            "-Disp", so they are easy to tell apart while keeping the build's
            prefix: MuseGadget-Disp in the open-source build, HomeLink-Disp in
            the internal one.

    config HOMEHUB_BUTTON_GPIO
        int "Active-low setup/reset button GPIO"
        range 0 28 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
        range 0 48
        default 28 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
        default 41
        help
            GPIO connected to the button, independently of the status LED.
            Uses an internal pull-up and treats a low level as pressed.
            Muse Home Link and the ESP32-C5 DevKitC-1 BOOT button both use GPIO28.

    config HOMEHUB_BUTTON_LABEL
        string "Button label shown in status prompts"
        default "BOOT"
        help
            The name shown in prompts like "Press <label> to confirm".
            Board overlays override this when the physical button has
            its own name.

    choice HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND
        prompt "Status LED hardware"
        default HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_PWM_RGB
        help
            Select the physical status LED implementation for this build. The
            Muse Home Link board uses a 3-channel PWM RGB LED. A stock
            ESP32-C5 DevKitC-1 has a single addressable RGB LED on GPIO27;
            other boards with one set HOMEHUB_LED_STRIP_GPIO.
            The always-on red power LED on the DevKitC-1 is not firmware
            controlled. The ideaspark ESP32 board and the SenseCAP Indicator
            have no status LED; their displays show the status colour in bars
            along the top and bottom edges, with an animation in between. The
            Home Assistant Voice PE shows the status colour on its LED ring.
            Seeed reSpeaker Lite shows it on a single RGB LED.
            The reTerminal E1001 and E1002 show a still status screen on
            their e-paper instead (black and white on the E1001, six colours
            on the E1002).
            Boards with the full UI have no status LED; their screen shows Link's pairing
            and connection state instead.

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_PWM_RGB
            bool "Muse Home Link PWM RGB LED"

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_DEVKIT_GPIO27
            bool "Addressable RGB LED (ESP32-C5 DevKitC-1 GPIO27 by default)"

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_IDEASPARK_ST7789
            bool "ideaspark ESP32 1.9 inch ST7789 display (status bars)"

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_WAVESHARE_C6_ST7789
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-C6-LCD-1.47 inch ST7789 display (status bars)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_SENSECAP_ST7701
            bool "SenseCAP Indicator 4 inch ST7701S RGB display (status bars)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && SPIRAM

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RETERMINAL_UC8179
            bool "reTerminal E1001 7.5 inch UC8179 e-paper (status screen)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && SPIRAM

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RETERMINAL_SPECTRA6
            bool "reTerminal E1002 7.3 inch Spectra 6 colour e-paper (status screen)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && SPIRAM

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_WAVESHARE_SSD1681
            bool "Waveshare ESP32-S3-1.54inch-ePaper SSD1681 e-paper (status screen)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3 && SPIRAM

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_VOICE_RING
            bool "Home Assistant Voice PE 12-LED ring"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RESPEAKER_LITE
            bool "Seeed reSpeaker Lite single RGB LED (experimental)"
            depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32S3

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_MUSE
            bool "Muse display"
            depends on MUSE_ENABLED

        config HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_NONE
            bool "No status LED"
    endchoice

    config HOMEHUB_LED_RGB_ORDER
        bool "Addressable LED takes red first (RGB order)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_DEVKIT_GPIO27
        default y
        help
            The LED on Espressif's ESP32-C5 DevKitC-1 (ESP32-C5-WROOM-1) takes
            red first, unlike the usual WS2812 green first (GRB). Turn this
            off if your board shows green as red and amber as green.

    config HOMEHUB_LED_STRIP_GPIO
        int "Addressable LED data GPIO"
        depends on HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_DEVKIT_GPIO27
        range 0 28 if IDF_TARGET_ESP32C5
        range 0 48
        default 27
        help
            GPIO driving the single addressable status LED. The ESP32-C5
            DevKitC-1 uses GPIO27. The build stops if the pin is one the chip
            uses for its SPI flash or PSRAM.

    config HOMEHUB_DISPLAY
        bool
        default y if HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_IDEASPARK_ST7789
        default y if HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_WAVESHARE_C6_ST7789
        default y if HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_SENSECAP_ST7701
        default y if HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RETERMINAL_UC8179
        default y if HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RETERMINAL_SPECTRA6
        default y if HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_WAVESHARE_SSD1681
        help
            Set for backends with a display: the status bars, animation and
            agent name (a still status screen on e-paper), and the display.*
            commands.

    config HOMEHUB_VOICE
        bool "Push-to-talk voice chat"
        depends on (HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_VOICE_RING || HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RESPEAKER_LITE) && SPIRAM
        default y
        help
            Hold the centre button (XIAO BOOT on reSpeaker Lite), speak,
            release: the audio goes to the agent's chat as a voice note,
            which the VM transcribes, and the
            reply appears in the Muse app as text. To speak it, plug a TTS
            API of your own into start_tts in muse_chat_session.cpp. This
            uses a second Noise session (Muse's voice session) with Link's
            account. While the mute
            switch is on, the button keeps its setup role (tap to confirm
            pairing, hold to reset).

    config HOMEHUB_SENSECAP_SENSORS
        bool "SenseCAP Indicator air sensors (sensors.read)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_SENSECAP_ST7701
        default y
        help
            Read the sensors the RP2040 reports over UART (GPIO 19/20) and
            answer sensors.read with the latest CO2, tVOC, temperature and
            humidity. The D1S and D1Pro have CO2 and tVOC built in;
            temperature and humidity need the Grove AHT20. Needs Seeed's stock
            RP2040 firmware.

    config HOMEHUB_RETERMINAL_SHT4X
        bool "reTerminal E100x onboard SHT4x sensor (sensors.read)"
        depends on HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RETERMINAL_UC8179 || HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_RETERMINAL_SPECTRA6
        default y
        help
            Poll the onboard Sensirion SHT4x temperature and humidity sensor
            over I2C (SDA GPIO19, SCL GPIO20, address 0x44) and answer
            sensors.read with the latest temperature and humidity.

    config HOMEHUB_RETERMINAL_SHT4X_TEMP_OFFSET
        int "SHT4x temperature offset, in tenths of a degree Celsius"
        depends on HOMEHUB_RETERMINAL_SHT4X
        default -33
        help
            Calibration offset added to the SHT4x temperature reading, in
            tenths of a degree Celsius. The onboard sensor reads warm from
            board heat; -33 (-3.3 C, about -6 F) is a starting point.

    config HOMEHUB_DISPLAY_COMMANDS
        bool
        default y if HOMEHUB_DISPLAY
        default y if HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_MUSE && SPIRAM
        help
            Set when display.draw_url can draw a downloaded image: the
            display backends, and boards with the full UI and PSRAM for the image.

    config HOMEHUB_PRE_DVT_GPIO
        bool "Use pre-DVT GPIO assignments"
        depends on HOMEHUB_LED_BACKEND_PWM_RGB
        default n
        help
            Select pre-DVT RGB LED pin mapping (R=24, G=25, B=26).
            DVT boards use R=2, G=3, B=6.

    config HOMEHUB_NVS_ENCRYPTION
        bool "Encrypt NVS with an HMAC-derived key"
        default n
        select NVS_ENCRYPTION
        select NVS_SEC_KEY_PROTECT_USING_HMAC
        help
            Encrypt the NVS partition using an HMAC key from eFuse block
            NVS_SEC_HMAC_EFUSE_KEY_ID (set in the NVS component menu). At
            first boot, if the chosen block is empty and read-protection can
            still be set, the application generates a key automatically.
            Boards with no HMAC key and a locked eFuse fall back to plaintext
            NVS. When this option is off, NVS is always plaintext regardless
            of eFuse state.

    config HOMEHUB_PAIRING_EFUSE_AUTH
        bool "Enable manufacturer eFuse pairing attestation"
        default n
        help
            Enable official v5 pairing with a protected manufacturer P-256 key.
            Requires hardware Secure Boot. An exactly unused key block selects
            explicit community v5 pairing; any malformed or unusable key fails
            closed. With this off, all pairing is community v5 with fresh ECDH
            encryption and physical confirmation but no manufacturer proof.

    config HOMEHUB_PAIRING_AUTH_EPOCH
        int "Manufacturer pairing public-key epoch"
        range 1 1
        default 1
        help
            Public-key epoch for official eFuse attestation only. Community
            pairing always advertises epoch zero and has no manufacturer key.

    config HOMEHUB_PAIRING_ECDSA_EFUSE_BLOCK
        int "Manufacturer pairing ECDSA eFuse key block"
        depends on HOMEHUB_PAIRING_EFUSE_AUTH
        default 6
        help
            The esp_efuse_block_t value of the read/write-protected ECDSA key:
            EFUSE_BLK_KEY0=4, KEY1=5, KEY2=6, ... KEY5=9.
            Manufacturing provisions the pairing key in KEY2 before Secure Boot
            locks read protection. No shared software attestation key is used.

endmenu
